[38;5;231m⏺[39m I explored the repo. Here's where I've landed before asking anything.

  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m "Logging" names a new subsystem — there's no existing logging layer to change, just
  scattered [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m calls. So this goes through the full process: questions → approaches → design → written spec →
  plan. If you think that's heavier than you want, say so and I'll adjust.

  [1mOne thing I want to flag up front[0m (before any code): [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a login form, and the current [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging [39m
  [38;5;153min:", username)[39m sits right next to a password field. Any logging subsystem I add here has to have an explicit rule
  about what never gets recorded — credentials, tokens, full form payloads. I'll treat redaction as a required part of
  the design, not an optional extra.

  [1mWhat's actually in the repo:[0m
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m + [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — browser login form, stubbed API call, [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m/[38;5;153mconsole.error[39m sprinkled in
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a separate Node entry point ([38;5;153mgreet('world')[39m)
  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m with no dependencies, no scripts, no test runner, no linter

  So "the app" could mean two different programs, and that fork changes nearly everything downstream:

  - [1mBrowser ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m)[0m — "production issues" means errors in a user's browser. Logs are useless unless they're shipped
    somewhere, so this pulls in a transport, a batching/failure story, and a real privacy surface (you'd be sending user
    data off-device). Expensive to change later; the transport decision is close to permanent.
  - [1mNode ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m)[0m — "production issues" means a server/CLI process. Logs go to stdout as structured JSON and
    something else collects them. Much cheaper, no network code, no data-egress question. Easy to swap the format later.
  - [1mBoth, behind one shared module[0m — a core logger with two thin adapters. Most work up front, but it's the only option
    that doesn't get rewritten if you later add the half you skipped.

  My recommendation: [1mNode-first[0m. It's where structured logging pays off immediately and where the privacy cost is
  lowest, and the browser side is a much larger conversation (you'd be picking an error-reporting service, not writing a
  logger).

